<h2>The occurrence and the FIR</h2>
<p>The FIR alleged that the accused, including the five petitioners, assaulted the informant Rabindra Dev and caused injuries. Petitioner no. 1 was alleged to have struck the informant on the head, petitioner no. 2 on the leg; the remaining petitioners faced allegations the defence characterised as general and omnibus. The genesis, as recorded in the order, was prosaic: a dispute over feeding cattle that escalated from words to blows — the classic reciprocal village occurrence that produces rival FIRs and decade-long trials.</p>

<h2>The three facts that decided the application</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Injury gradation</strong><br>Annexure-2, the injury report, showed simple lacerations against the petitioners' alleged acts.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Attribution of the grievous injury</strong><br>The sole grievous injury was ascribed to co-accused Chunnu — not any petitioner.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No coercive process</strong><br>The trial court's report confirmed no chargesheet and no steps under Sections 82–83 CrPC despite the FIR dating to 2018.</div>
</div>
<p>To these the petitioners added clean antecedents, an explanation for the delay — they were working outside — and an undertaking to cooperate with the investigation and trial. The State's opposition rested on the FIR allegations themselves, which the court weighed against the medical record rather than accepting at face value.</p>

<h2>Reading the order in the law's framework</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail in group-clash cases consistently turns on individualisation. Sections 147, 148 and 149 IPC permit constructive liability at trial, but at the bail stage courts distinguish between the accused who allegedly wielded the decisive blow and those swept in by membership of the crowd. The order also illustrates the significance of the coercive-process check: before granting pre-arrest bail in older FIRs, the Patna High Court routinely calls for a report from the court below, and the presence or absence of proclamation under Sections 82–83 CrPC frequently decides whether the applicant is treated as a fugitive or as a person the process never pursued.</p>

<h2>Conditions and enforcement</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Surrender window:</strong> the protection operates on arrest or surrender before the court below within four weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Bonds:</strong> Rs 10,000 each with two sureties of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the concerned court.</li>
  <li><strong>Family surety:</strong> one bailor per petitioner must be a family member or close relative — Patna's standard traceability device.</li>
  <li><strong>Cooperation clause with a complainant trigger:</strong> if the petitioners fail to cooperate, the informant herself may apply to cancel the bonds — a notable allocation of enforcement initiative to the first party.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In reciprocal village-clash FIRs, the anticipatory bail brief should be built as a table: each petitioner, the act attributed, the injury it allegedly caused, and the medical gradation of that injury — with the trial court's report on chargesheet and process status annexed. Where the grievous injury belongs to someone else's alleged act, that single row of the table usually carries the application.</p>
</div>
